From a58328674dcdb5873281623ca41707039faf3e48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: r9 <r9@local>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 15:49:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] hasvk: 32-bit softpin layout for Cherry Trail (4 GiB
 PPGTT): real BDA / Vulkan 1.3

Cherry Trail was kept on relocations because hasvk's softpin address
layout needs 48-bit addresses (pools spread over ~15 GiB of VMA) and CHV's
full PPGTT is exactly 4 GiB, which is also why supports_48bit_addresses is
false there.  The kernel accepts EXEC_OBJECT_PINNED at any page-aligned
address below 4 GiB, and nothing in the gfx8 softpin code depends on 48-bit
addresses: the vertex cache workaround only needs to know the real
addresses (and can never trigger when every address is below 4 GiB).

Make the layout a runtime choice (struct anv_va_layout in the physical
device): the existing 48-bit one when supports_48bit_addresses, otherwise a
32-bit one with the state pools in the low 1 GiB (general 2-64 MiB, dynamic
64-256 MiB, binding tables 256-384 MiB, surface states 384-640 MiB,
instructions 640-768 MiB, client-visible heap for capture/replay 768 MiB -
1 GiB) and one heap from 1 GiB to 4 GiB - 64 MiB.  The binding table pool
stays below the surface state pool within 4 GiB (16-bit binding table and
32-bit surface state offsets from Surface State Base Address), the general
state base address stays 0.  The state pools get a max_size so that they
cannot grow into their neighbour; STATE_BASE_ADDRESS sizes, the batch
decoder bases and the shader constant data address come from the layout.
The Vulkan heap is sized from the address space (2.94 GiB - 256 MiB)
instead of the 2 GiB clamp relocation mode gets on CHV.  Relocation mode
stays available for comparison with HASVK_USE_RELOCATIONS=1 (and is still
what gfx7 uses).

With softpin, hasvk's existing Broadwell paths switch on: A64 buffer access
(VK_KHR/EXT_buffer_device_address, bufferDeviceAddress, 64-bit global
SSBO/UBO addressing, 65535 SSBOs), secondary command buffer calls and
command buffer chaining, no per-submit surface state relocations, and the
1.3 apiVersion/conformanceVersion.

Tested on a GPD Win (Atom x7-Z8700): dEQP-VK.binding_model.buffer_device_address.*
4716/4716 pass, sampled groups identical to the relocation-mode driver,
DXVK 2.7.1 (with the descriptor indexing option) renders correctly, submit
CPU time -38 % user / -13 % sys in a copy benchmark.
